.TH CHECK-CERT 1 "2026-10-20" "SCD Reporting v02.05" "SCD Reporting"
.SH NAME
check-cert \- check the SCD Reporting TLS certificate and reissue it through cert-manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./scripts/check-cert.sh
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B check-cert.sh
connects to the application hostname, retrieves the certificate chain the OKD
router is serving, and checks:
.IP \(bu 2
that the subjectAltName contains the hostname (a mismatch usually means the
router is answering with its wildcard default; see the router sharding section
of
.BR helm/simple/README.md );
.IP \(bu 2
that the certificate has not expired and does not expire within
.I DAYS
days;
.IP \(bu 2
revocation status, by OCSP against the responder in the Authority Information
Access extension, falling back to the CRL distribution point;
.IP \(bu 2
with cluster access, that the cert-manager
.B Certificate
is Ready and that the serial in its TLS secret matches the served serial.
.PP
With
.B \-\-reissue
a failed check triggers a new issuance. The script changes the
.B cert-manager.io/issuer-name
annotation on the TLS secret, which trips cert-manager's issuer-mismatch
trigger. The old certificate stays in the secret and on the Route until the new
one is written, so the site keeps serving throughout. This is used in place of
.B "cmctl renew"
because that writes the
.B certificates/status
subresource, which namespace users cannot do on okdprod.
.PP
Before reissuing, the script sets
.B spec.privateKey.rotationPolicy=Always
if it is not already set. With a reused key the CSR is identical to the
previous order and the InCommon ACME server rejects it with
.BR "badCSR: CSR of an existing certificate order" .
.PP
cert-manager holds off 2^(n\-1) hours (maximum 32 h) after the n-th failed
issuance, and that backoff cannot be cleared without status access. In that case
.B \-\-reissue
prints when cert-manager will retry on its own and exits 3.
.PP
.B \-\-recreate
gets around the backoff. cert-manager keeps the failure count and time only in
the Certificate's
.BR .status ,
so a newly created Certificate has none. The script:
.IP 1. 4
refuses if the TLS secret has an ownerReference to the Certificate (cert-manager
started with
.BR \-\-enable\-certificate\-owner\-ref ),
because deleting the Certificate would then garbage-collect the secret and the
Route would lose its certificate;
.IP 2. 4
saves the live Certificate as JSON in the backup directory;
.IP 3. 4
writes a manifest without status and server-set metadata, keeping the Helm
labels and
.B meta.helm.sh/*
annotations so Helm still owns the object, and with
.BR rotationPolicy=Always ;
.IP 4. 4
validates that manifest, changes the secret's issuer annotation (so the new
Certificate reissues instead of adopting the certificate already in the secret),
deletes the Certificate and creates it again.
.PP
The secret's certificate data and the Route are never changed, so the old certificate is served until
the new one is written. CertificateRequest numbering restarts at
.BR <name>\-1 ,
and the old requests and ACME orders are garbage-collected with the old Certificate.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-H " HOSTNAME"
Hostname to check. Default:
.BR $SCD_HOSTNAME ,
or
.BR scd-reporting.fnal.gov .
.TP
.BI \-n " NAMESPACE"
OKD namespace. Default:
.BR $SCD_NAMESPACE ,
or
.BR scd-reporting .
.TP
.BI \-c " CERTIFICATE"
cert-manager Certificate name. Default:
.BR $SCD_CERT_NAME ,
or
.BR scd-reporting .
.TP
.BI \-d " DAYS"
Report a problem if the certificate expires within this many days. Default:
.BR 21 .
.TP
.B \-\-reissue
Reissue if any check fails, wait for the new certificate, and re-run the checks.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Reissue even if every check passes. Implies
.BR \-\-reissue .
.TP
.B \-\-recreate
Reissue by deleting and recreating the Certificate object, which clears
cert-manager's failure backoff (see DESCRIPTION). Implies
.BR \-\-reissue ;
combine with
.B \-\-force
to recreate when every check passes. Needs
.BR python3 .
.TP
.BI \-b " DIR"
Directory where
.B \-\-recreate
saves the JSON backup of the Certificate. Default:
.BR $SCD_CERT_BACKUP_DIR ,
or
.BR $TMPDIR .
.TP
.BI \-w " SECONDS"
How long to wait for cert-manager to issue. Default:
.BR 600 .
.TP
.B \-\-no-cluster
Only inspect the served certificate; do not call
.BR oc .
Needs no OKD login.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Print the
.B oc
commands a reissue would run, without running them.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short help message and exit.
.PP
Command-line options override the environment variables.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Certificate is good, or a reissue completed and the new certificate is good.
.TP
.B 1
A problem was found (revoked, expired or expiring, wrong name, Certificate not
Ready, secret and served serial differ).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, missing tool, or not logged in to OKD.
.TP
.B 3
A reissue was requested but did not complete (failure, timeout, or
cert-manager backoff).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SCD_HOSTNAME
.TQ
.B SCD_NAMESPACE
.TQ
.B SCD_CERT_NAME
Defaults for
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B SCD_CERT_BACKUP_DIR
Default for
.BR \-b .
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the production certificate:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/check-cert.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check and reissue if revoked or expiring:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/check-cert.sh --reissue
.EE
.RE
.PP
A reissue failed and cert-manager is in backoff; recreate the Certificate to
retry now, keeping the backup in the repository's ignored
.B logs/
directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/check-cert.sh --recreate -b logs
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show what a forced recreate would do without changing anything:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/check-cert.sh --recreate --force --dry-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check from a machine without OKD access, warning 30 days ahead (suitable for cron;
non-zero exit means action is needed):
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/check-cert.sh --no-cluster -d 30
.EE
.RE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.BR bash ,
.BR openssl ,
.BR curl ,
and for cluster checks
.B oc
logged in to the cluster. On Windows 11 run it under WSL or Git Bash.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scd\-check\-cert (1)
(the unattended in-cluster monitor that mails alerts),
.BR deploy (1),
.BR restart-pod (1),
.BR oc (1),
.BR openssl-ocsp (1)
